# Database
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.dump
*.sql
# …but migrations MUST be tracked — the startup runner ships + applies them.
!migrations/*.sql
# …and so must their downgrades. This second exception is NOT redundant:
# `!migrations/*.sql` does not match a subdirectory, so without this line
# `migrations/downgrades/*.sql` stays swallowed by `*.sql` above and a downgrade
# is silently absent from the commit that adds its migration — you only find out
# when you need to roll back. The downgrades live one level down precisely
# BECAUSE `apply_migrations.py` globs `migrations/*.sql` non-recursively: a
# downgrade beside its migration would sort right after it and undo it on the
# same boot.
!migrations/downgrades/*.sql
# …and so must test-fixture SQL. The schema-parity gate's genesis shim lives
# here; without this exception it is silently absent in CI and the gate fails on
# a missing input rather than on a real divergence.
!tests/support/*.sql

